**C program to reverse a string**

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
void reverse(char s[]) {
    int i, len = strlen(s);
    char temp;
    for (i = 0; i < len / 2; i++) {
        temp = s[i];
        s[i] = s[len - 1 - i];
        s[len - 1 - i] = temp;
    }
}
int main() {
    char s[50];
    printf("Enter a string: ");
    scanf("%s", s);
    reverse(s);
    printf("Reversed string: %s\n", s);
    return 0;
}
```
Example: input `"Diploma"` → output `"amolpiD"`.

The algorithm swaps characters symmetrically from both ends toward the middle using a temporary variable. Only `len/2` swaps are needed.

**Mark distribution — Total 6 marks: a) 2 marks + b) 4 marks (program).**

**a) Pointer to a function**

A **pointer to a function** stores the memory address of a function instead of a data value. It allows a function to be passed as an argument to another function or selected dynamically at run time.

```c
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int (*fptr)(int, int) = add;   // fptr points to add
int result = fptr(3, 4);        // calls add → 7
```

**b) Access a one-dimensional array using a pointer**

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int *p = arr;               // p points to the first element
    int i;
    printf("Array elements:\n");
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        printf("%d ", *(p + i));   // pointer arithmetic
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```
Output: `10 20 30 40 50`

`arr` decays to the address of its first element. `*(p + i)` accesses the i-th element; `p + i` moves the pointer by `i × sizeof(int)` bytes.

